  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 1245 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nUpper-level heights will peak late Tuesday night into Wednesday\nmorning. Thus, Wednesday looks to be like another oppressively \nhot day, with additional heat headlines likely needed for most\nlocations.\n\nRidging begins to elongate and flatten late Wednesday afternoon \nand evening as a robust trough swings across the Northern Plains \nand Upper Midwest. Models and ensembles continue to be in good \nagreement in pushing a modest cold front into the area, but exact \ntiming and just how far south it is able to push is still a little\nmurky. Regardless, confidence continues to increase regarding the\nfront bringing in widespread cloud coverage and rain chances \nWednesday afternoon/evening and lingering into Friday before the \nfront lifts back northward and southerly flow returns. The front \nis anticipated to stall somewhere near the the I-40 corridor \nduring the daytime Thursday, per latest guidance. This may change.\nTemperatures are expected to drop closer to seasonal average \nbehind the frontal boundary on Thursday, which should feel \ndrastically more comfortable in comparison to recent days. Out ahead\nof the front, temperatures will remain unseasonably hot, but \ncloud cover should keep temperatures from reaching triple digits, \nat least.\n\nNWP models begin to diverge with solutions by the end of the week\nand into the upcoming weekend. However, there is good consensus \nthat the FA stays on the eastern periphery of the ridge and just \non the southern periphery of a longwave trough before ridging \nbuilds back in and becomes the more dominate feature again by \nSunday. Both GFS and ECMWF try to bring another weak frontal \nboundary Saturday afternoon/evening, stalling the front near the \nKS/OK border by Saturday evening, and then lifting it back north \non Sunday morning. Needless to say, confidence of this occurring \nis low at this time, given the inconsistencies in the models \nrecently. Overall, the good news is that the general trend in the \nlong-term seems to be slightly wetter and slightly cooler compared\nto recent days and weeks.\n\nMejia",
